WHAT THE RESPELL IS FOR. Both spellings parse. TraiT is `NamE Modifier* Limit?
TraiTdata?` and TraiTdata is `'=' DatA Modifier* Limit?`, so a dash written after
the `=` lands in TraiTdata's Modifier* instead of TraiT's. aCTionTraiTdata modifies
the DatA node; aCTionTraiT then setContent's that node onto the trait, and
setContent does not carry flags (bear-trap #1/#2). So the old spelling applies the
dash to a node whose flags are about to be discarded, and the term is minted as a
labelled rule with NO rStuff -- the bare-master wreckage the audit has been
reporting. The respell fixes that, and for four of the five rules it fixes it with
no other effect at all.

⚠ THE BISECT. Five grammars, one respelled line each, one run apiece, same bare
binary. The wreckage removal is ADDITIVE AND PER-RULE; the walk regression is NOT.

    respelled rule    oneTest MISSRULE    iterT1 / iterT1m
    HEAD (none)              12                MATCH
    BlocK                    10                DIFF  <- this file
    Braced                   10                MATCH
    Parens                   10                MATCH
    StringXP                 12                MATCH
    ScopeXP                  12                MATCH
    all five                  6                DIFF

Braced and Parens each remove their own pair of phantoms and nothing else moves.
StringXP and ScopeXP remove no phantoms -- `pound` and `colon` were never in the
MISSRULE population -- and nothing else moves for them either. BlocK removes its
pair AND takes the walk.

WHAT THE WALK DOES. incant/iterT1 nests: trunk holds leafA and leafB, each leaf
holds two entries, and the four entries carry no list so the third level bottoms
out on iterate's no-list refusal. It expects, in this order:

    at trunk / at leafA / at i / at j / at leafB / at k / at l      = 7

With the BlocK respell it reads FIVE:

    at trunk / at leafA / at i /          at leafB / at k

`at j` and `at l` are gone -- the enclosing loop stops after the first member that
refuses -- and the refusal count goes 4 to 2 for the same reason. iterT1m is the
same shape and moves the same way. Both are diff-based fleet rows and both go red.

