THE CAUSE. aCTionBlocK handed back NULL for a block that SUCCEEDED AND YIELDED
NOTHING, where labelNO was owed. GroupControl.twk:158 already said it --
"NULL = failed · labelNO = succeeded and yields NOTHING · any other node ..." --
and four sites in ruleActions.rtn already ended `if !result result = labelNO;`,
including aCTionWhilE itself. aCTionBlocK was the one that did not.

THE CONSUMER. aCTionWhilE's loop is

    if result = StatemenT.gMethod(StatemenT) { ...branch handling... }
    else    break;

so a body that succeeded and yielded nothing STOPPED THE LOOP. walk(i) hits the
no-list refusal, runAction clears the arm and returns null, the body's block hands
back null, and the caller's `else break;` reads it as "stop".

⚠ FIFTH MEASURED MEMBER OF ONE-CHANNEL-ONE-MEANING. `result` carried the body's
VALUE and the loop's CONTINUE SIGNAL on one slot. `broke` is the second channel and
is the whole repair: a block that BROKE on a refusal or a branch still hands back
what it had, because there null means failed and that is true.

⚠ AND IT WAS MASKED, WHICH IS WHY IT LOOKED LIKE A GRAMMAR DEFECT FOR THREE
SESSIONS. While BlocK's curlies carried labels, the trailing rightCurly was the
block's value on EVERY iteration, so no block could ever hand back null and the
loop could not stop that way. Honouring the noLabel dash removed the sentinel and
left the channel exposed. THE DASH WAS ALWAYS RIGHT; the sentinel was hiding the
defect behind it.

THE A/B THAT NAMED IT, measured at the block's foot with measureBlockResult:

    curlies LABELLED   items=5   while-body result = rightCurly   loop CONTINUES
    curlies noLabel    items=3   while-body result = NULL         loop STOPS

